# What do the pawns tell you?
teaches: ["strategy/pawn-structure"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What are isolated, doubled, backward and passed pawns good and bad for? How does a pawn chain tell you which side of the board to play on?"
next: {"title":"Which of your pieces is doing nothing?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/20-which-of-your-pieces-is-doing-nothing.cast"}

## Warm-up: traps and guards
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b1kbnr/pppp1Npp/8/8/2BnP3/8/PPPP1PqP/RNBQKR2 b Qkq - 1 6, moves: [Qxe4+, Qe2, Nxe2], flip: true}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/1p3ppp/p4n2/3b2B1/8/P5P1/1P3P1P/3R1BK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6, gxf6, Rxd5], highlights: [f7, f6]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: bst
      title: Blackburne Shilling Gambit
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nd4 4.Nxe5? Qg5 5.Nxf7 Qxg2 6.Rf1 Qxe4+ 7.Be2 Nf3# is the trap's mate.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackburne_Shilling_Gambit

    card title "Warm-up: traps and guards"

A: Today's question: what do the pawns tell you about your plan? First, two warm-ups, starting on Black's side.
    draw position

A: This is yesterday's Blackburne–Shilling trap, after your queen took g2.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_g2

B: The rook on f1 is right there. I take it.
    highlight piece_f1

A: It's guarded twice, by the king and the bishop: you'd lose the queen.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_c4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","Which pawn can your queen take with check?"] themes ["trap"] voice a teaches ["openings/traps"]
        right: Queen takes e4, check. A bishop block walks into knight to f3, mate; so White blocks with the queen, and the knight takes it.
        wrong: Look for a queen check that also takes something.

B: So that knight was the danger all along.
    highlight piece_e2

A: Second one, back on White's side: who is guarding what?
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: My rook attacks the bishop on d5. I take it.
    highlight piece_d1 piece_d5

A: Then the knight takes back, and you've given a rook for a bishop.
    highlight piece_f6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Remove the guard.","Your bishop on g5."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a teaches ["tactics/removing-the-defender"]
        right: Bishop takes f6: the bishop on d5 loses its only guard. The pawn takes back, and the rook takes d5.
        wrong: Before you take on d5, take away what guards it.

A: Now look at Black's pawns: two on the f-file.
    draw hl_f7 hl_f6 parallel true

A: The pieces will come and go; that damage stays. That's today.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_f6

## Four kinds of pawn, and the chain
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2rr2k1/pp2bppp/4pn2/8/3P4/3B1N2/PP3PPP/2R1R1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nd5], arrows: [{from: c1, to: c7}, {from: e1, to: e5}]}
      - {fen: 4k3/1pp2ppp/p1p5/8/4P3/8/PPP2PPP/4K3 w - - 0 1, moves: [], highlights: [e4, f2, g2, h2, a6, b7, c7, c6]}
      - {fen: 3rr1k1/pp2bppp/3p1n2/4p3/2P1P3/2N5/PP2BPPP/3RR1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [d5, cxd5], arrows: [{from: c4, to: d5}, {from: e4, to: d5}]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3pP3/3P4/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3, moves: [c5], arrows: [{from: c5, to: d4}], highlights: [d4, e5]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: nimzo
      title: My System
      authors: Aron Nimzowitsch
      year: 1925
      finding: Passed and weak pawns are to be blockaded by a piece in front of them (first restrain, then blockade, then destroy); a pawn chain is attacked at its base.
    - id: euwe
      title: Ruy Lopez, Exchange Variation
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Max Euwe analysed the pure pawn ending of the Exchange Variation (White a2 b2 c2 e4 f2 g2 h2, Black a6 b7 c7 c6 f7 g7 h7) as good for White: White can make a kingside passed pawn, while Black''s doubled c-pawns cannot make one on the queenside.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruy_Lopez,_Exchange_Variation

    card title "Four kinds of pawn, and the chain"

A: Pawns never move backwards, so damage like that usually stays. Start with a pawn that stands alone.
    draw position

A: Here, White's d-pawn has no pawn on either side of it: an isolated pawn.
    highlight piece_d4

B: Looks fine to me. It's in the centre.
    point at.ref piece_d4 gesture circle

A: In the middlegame it can be: space, and open files beside it for the rooks.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: But no pawn can ever guard it, and no pawn can chase a piece from the square in front of it.
    point at.ref sq_d5 gesture circle

A: So where can the knight stand, safe from every pawn?
    animate box.x 20 box.y 120 box.w 560 box.h 530 duration 0.6

    quiz "Which square can the knight reach that no white pawn can ever attack?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 890 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: d5, the square in front of the isolated pawn: no white pawn can ever chase the knight from there, and it blocks the pawn. On e4 or g4, a pawn push sends it away.
        wrong: Which square can no white pawn ever attack?
        + d5
        * e4
        * g4

    animate box.x 170 box.y 25 box.w 660 box.h 650 duration 0.6

B: So the weakness is the square in front, as much as the pawn.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Nimzowitsch called this a blockade: stop the pawn with a piece first, attack it later.
    highlight piece_d5

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Doubled pawns next: two on one file. Black took them on in the opening, for the two bishops.
    highlight piece_c7 piece_c6

B: Seven pawns each, no pieces. That's a draw.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_e8

A: Count by wing: each side has four against three, White on the kingside, Black on the queenside.
    draw hl_e4 hl_f2 hl_g2 hl_h2 hl_a6 hl_b7 hl_c7 hl_c6 parallel true

A: So which side can make a passed pawn?
    animate box.x 20 box.y 120 box.w 560 box.h 530 duration 0.6

    quiz "Which side can make a passed pawn?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 890 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: White. A passed pawn has no enemy pawn in front or beside it, and four healthy pawns against three make one. Black's four include the doubled pair, and they can't get past three.
        wrong: Can the pawn on c7 ever help the one on c6 get past?
        + White
        * Black
        * Both

    animate box.x 170 box.y 25 box.w 660 box.h 650 duration 0.6

A: Max Euwe showed why this ending is good for White: only White can make a passed pawn.
    highlight piece_e4 piece_f2 piece_g2 piece_h2

    erase hl_e4 hl_f2 hl_g2 hl_h2 hl_a6 hl_b7 hl_c7 hl_c6

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: A backward pawn: Black's c-pawn is gone and the e-pawn went ahead, so no pawn can guard d6.
    highlight piece_d6

A: And White's pawns watch the square in front.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

B: Then I push it to d5, and it isn't backward any more.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: White takes, and Black can't win the pawn back: d5 has more white guards than black attackers.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    animate plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: So it stays on d6, on an open file for White's rooks,
    highlight piece_d6 piece_d1

A: with d5 a home for White's knight.
    point at.ref sq_d5 gesture circle

    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Last, chains: pawns guarding each other on a diagonal.
    draw hl_d4 hl_e5 parallel true

A: White's chain points at the kingside, and White plays there.
    point at.ref sq_g6 gesture circle

B: So Black defends the king and waits?
    highlight piece_e8

A: Black plays on the other wing, where his own chain points: pawn to c5.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Nimzowitsch's advice: attack a chain at its base. Here the base is d4.
    draw arrow_0

A: As a guide, not a law: play on the side your pawns point to.
    highlight piece_d4

## Your turn: make the pawns talk
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 6k1/8/8/8/ppp5/8/PPP5/7K b - - 0 1, moves: [b3, axb3, c3, bxc3, a3], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3r4/pp1P1pkp/6p1/1B5n/8/8/5PPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rc8, Rxd7, Bxd7]}
      - {fen: 3r1rk1/ppp1pppp/2n1b3/8/3PP3/5N2/PP3PPP/2BR1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [d5]}
      - {fen: R7/P4k2/6p1/5p1p/7P/6P1/r4PK1/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh8, Rxa7, Rh7+, Ke6, Rxa7]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/pppnnpbp/3p2p1/3Pp3/2P1P3/2NN4/PP2BPPP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 4 10, moves: [f5], arrows: [{from: c4, to: c5}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: nimzo
      title: My System
      authors: Aron Nimzowitsch
      year: 1925
      finding: A pawn chain is attacked at its base.

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you can read the pawns, make them work. First, from Black's side.
    draw position

B: Three pawns against three. Nobody's passed.
    highlight piece_a4 piece_b4 piece_c4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Make a passed pawn." widget chess line true hints ["Give a pawn to open a file.","Start with the middle pawn."] themes ["pawn structure"] voice a
        right: Pawn to b3. White takes, pawn to c3 hits again, White takes, and the a-pawn runs: White's king is too far away.
        wrong: Which pawn move forces White to open a file for one of yours?

A: Back to White. A pawn on the seventh, and a rook in its way.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: The rook on d8 blocks it. I'll bring my king up.
    highlight piece_d8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Attack the blocker.","Your rook on c1."] themes ["deflection","pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["tactics/deflection","strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Rook to c8 attacks the blocker. If it takes, the pawn takes and queens; so Black gives the rook for the pawn, and the bishop takes back.
        wrong: Which move hits the rook on d8, so that taking costs Black the pawn's queen?

A: A king walk is too slow; one move did it. Next, a quiet position.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Black's pieces look safe to me.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_e6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn can attack two pieces.","Your pawn on d4."] themes ["fork","pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Pawn to d5 forks the knight and the bishop, and the pawn on e4 guards it: one of them is lost.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two black pieces at once?

B: The cheapest attacker wins: look at the pawns, not only the pieces.
    highlight piece_d5

A: One older theme: a passed pawn on a7, and a rook behind it.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Get your rook out of the pawn's way.","If the rook takes a7, which check lines up the king and the rook?"] themes ["skewer","pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["tactics/skewer","strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Rook to h8, so the pawn threatens to queen. The rook takes a7, rook to h7 is check along the seventh, the king steps away, and the rook on a7 falls.
        wrong: Move your rook so the pawn can queen, and keep a check in reserve.

A: Now a plan, not a tactic. A closed centre, and Black to move.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Which break fits Black's chain?
    animate box.x 20 box.y 120 box.w 560 box.h 530 duration 0.6

    quiz "Which pawn break fits Black's chain?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 890 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: f5. Black's chain points at the kingside, so pawn to f5 hits White's chain at its base, e4.
        wrong: Which way do the pawns on d6 and e5 point?
        + f5
        * b5
        * c6

    animate box.x 170 box.y 25 box.w 660 box.h 650 duration 0.6

B: And White goes the other way?
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Yes: pawn to c5, at Black's base on d6. Each side races on its own wing.
    draw arrow_0

A: So read the pawns before you choose a plan:
    highlight piece_d5 piece_e4

A: holes to sit on, majorities that make passed pawns, and the side the chain points to.
    highlight piece_d6 piece_e5

    erase position arrow_0

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 500 font_size 110

A: Then find its first mistake, and name one pawn weakness in it.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 360 font_size 76
    text hw3 "1 pawn weakness" x 500 y 250 font_size 76
    parallel true

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:20 title "Which of your pieces is doing nothing?" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:19 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "19 of 30" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
